  "questions": [
    {
      "source_id": "1",
      "stem": "A survey drone is located at the origin of an $xy$-coordinate system. At a certain instant, a weather balloon is at position $\\vec r = -8\\hat{i}+15\\hat{j}$, where distances are measured in kilometers. Which of the following statements is correct?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "The distance between the balloon and the drone is $23\\text{ km}$.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This comes from adding the component magnitudes, $8+15=23$, instead of finding the vector magnitude. Distance from the origin in two dimensions must be computed with the Pythagorean relation, not simple addition."
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "The distance between the balloon and the drone is $17\\text{ km}$.",
          "is_correct": true,
          "explanation": "The distance from the origin is the magnitude of the position vector. Using $|\\vec r|=\\sqrt{(-8)^2+15^2}=\\sqrt{64+225}=\\sqrt{289}=17\\text{ km}$, the correct statement is that the balloon is $17\\text{ km}$ away. The negative sign on the $x$-component affects direction, not the final distance."
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "The distance between the balloon and the drone is $15\\text{ km}$.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This mistake uses only the $y$-component and ignores the horizontal displacement. In two-dimensional motion, both components contribute to the distance from the origin."
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "The distance between the balloon and the drone is $8\\text{ km}$.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This mistake uses only the magnitude of the $x$-component. The full distance must include both perpendicular components of the position vector."
        }
      ],
      "question_type": "multiple_choice",
      "skipped": false,
      "skip_reason": "",
      "answer": "",
      "parts": []
    },
    {
      "source_id": "2",
      "stem": "The position of a particle moving along the $x$-axis is given by $\\vec s(t)=\\left[(0.90\\,\\text{m/s}^3)t^3+(4.2\\,\\text{m/s}^2)t^2\\right]\\hat{i}$. Which of the following statements is correct?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "This motion has constant acceleration.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This error comes from assuming any polynomial position function leads to constant acceleration. Because the position contains a $t^3$ term, the second derivative still depends on $t$, so the acceleration is not constant."
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "The magnitude of the acceleration at $t=0$ is $4.2\\,\\text{m/s}^2$.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This mistake reads the coefficient of the $t^2$ term directly as the acceleration. The acceleration must be found by differentiating the position function twice."
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "The magnitude of the acceleration at $t=0$ is $8.4\\,\\text{m/s}^2$.",
          "is_correct": true,
          "explanation": "Differentiate the position to get velocity: $\\vec v(t)=\\dfrac{d\\vec s}{dt}=\\left[(2.7)t^2+8.4t\\right]\\hat{i}$. Differentiating again gives acceleration, $\\vec a(t)=\\dfrac{d\\vec v}{dt}=\\left[5.4t+8.4\\right]\\hat{i}$, so at $t=0$ the magnitude is $8.4\\,\\text{m/s}^2$. The motion therefore has time-dependent acceleration, but its initial acceleration is nonzero."
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "The magnitude of the acceleration is $5.4\\,\\text{m/s}^2$.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This comes from confusing the coefficient of the linear term in $a(t)$ with the actual acceleration value. Since $a(t)=5.4t+8.4$, the acceleration depends on time and is not always $5.4\\,\\text{m/s}^2$."
        }
      ],
      "question_type": "multiple_choice",
      "skipped": false,
      "skip_reason": "",
      "answer": "",
      "parts": []
    },
    {
      "source_id": "3",
      "stem": "A robot moves for $4.0\\,$s from position $\\vec r_A = 1\\hat{i}-7\\hat{j}$ to position $\\vec r_B = -5\\hat{i}+1\\hat{j}$, where positions are measured in meters. Which of the following statements is correct?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "The acceleration at $t=0$ must be $0.63\\,\\text{m/s}^2$.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This is a misconception because acceleration cannot be uniquely determined from only initial position, final position, and elapsed time. Many different motions with different accelerations could connect the same two points in $4.0\\,$s."
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "None of the above is correct.",
          "is_correct": true,
          "explanation": "From the given data, the displacement is $\\Delta\\vec r=\\vec r_B-\\vec r_A=(-6)\\hat{i}+8\\hat{j}$, whose magnitude is $|\\Delta\\vec r|=\\sqrt{(-6)^2+8^2}=10\\,\\text{m}$. However, this $10\\,\\text{m}$ is the displacement magnitude, not necessarily the distance traveled, and the information provided is insufficient to determine the instantaneous speed or acceleration at $t=0$. Therefore none of the other statements must be true."
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "The distance traveled by the robot is $10\\,$m.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This confuses displacement magnitude with path length. The straight-line displacement is $10\\,$m, but the actual distance traveled depends on the path and cannot be known from the endpoints alone."
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "The speed at $t=0$ must be $2.5\\,\\text{m/s}$.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This comes from dividing the displacement magnitude by the total time, which gives only a rough average based on endpoint information. Instantaneous speed at the initial moment cannot be determined without more information about the motion."
        }
      ],
      "question_type": "multiple_choice",
      "skipped": false,
      "skip_reason": "",
      "answer": "",
      "parts": []
    }
